n":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_header","orig":"Minireview","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":741.6666666666666,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":40.0,"r":74.0,"t":749.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/0","text":"Minireview"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_header","orig":"9 { 0 9 ẵ","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":273.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":562.3333333333334,"r":575.6666666666666,"t":476.6543298605706},"charspan":[0,9],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"9 { 0 9 ẵ"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"TABLE 1 Summary of features and experimental observations for selected multicargo T3SC","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":705.6666666666666,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":39.666666666666664,"r":353.6666666666667,"t":718.3333333333334},"charspan":[0,86],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"TABLE 1 Summary of features and experimental observations for selected multicargo T3SC"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Listed by direct interactions in experimental binding assays. Additional roles in effector secretion without demonstrated binding have been observed in some cases.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":587.3333333333334,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":43.333333333333336,"r":500.0,"t":598.3333333333334},"charspan":[0,163],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Listed by direct interactions in experimental binding assays. Additional roles in effector secretion without demonstrated binding have been observed in some cases."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"footnote","orig":"ND, not determined.","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":579.6666666666666,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":44.0,"r":104.0,"t":587.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,19],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"ND, not determined."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"regardless of gene context. Within the last 10 years; number of multicargo chaperones have been implicated in binding, recruitment; regulatory; and docking roles. The importance ofthese pro teins is validated by findings where multicargo chaperone mutants exhibit significant virulence defects in animal and plant models of infection. The features of some of these chaperones are presented below and summarized in Table 1","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":476.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":38.333333333333336,"r":290.0,"t":554.3333333333334},"charspan":[0,421],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"regardless of gene context. Within the last 10 years; number of multicargo chaperones have been implicated in binding, recruitment; regulatory; and docking roles. The importance ofthese pro teins is validated by findings where multicargo chaperone mutants exhibit significant virulence defects in animal and plant models of infection. The features of some of these chaperones are presented below and summarized in Table 1"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"InvB. The invABC genetic locus located within the Salmonella pathogenicity island 1 (SPI-1) was initially implicated in eukary otic cell invasion although invB mutants were still able to invade cells in vitro; making it difficult to assess the contribution of InvB to pathogenesis (22). invB mutants were shown to have a virulence defect in an avian model of Salmonella infection; yet the exact contribution or functional role of InvB in virulence remained un known 63). At the sequence level, InvB shares 31% sequence similarity with Spal5 (SpaK), a protein encoded in the mxi-spa genetic locus associated with Shigella host cell invasion; and there fore a link to Salmonella invasion was suggested. The first demon stration for InvB being a chaperone was a study that used a yeast two-hybrid approach and in vitro infection experiments. Bronstein et al. 6) discovered that InvB interacts with SspA (SipA) and contributes to efficient translocation of SipA into target host cells. Two groups then answered question relating to Salmonella effectors encoded outside SPI-1 by demonstrating that InvB was required for SopEand SopE2 secretion and translocation into host strain, InvB was shown to interact with SopA (20). Aside from SopE and SopE2, the InvB interacting effectors do not share extensive sequence similarity which raised the important question of InvB mediates specificity to effector partner proteins. As it turns out, intrinsic structural features within effectors are critical for InvB binding (45) (presented below). key how binding","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":198.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":38.333333333333336,"r":288.3333333333333,"t":476.0},"charspan":[0,1547],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"InvB. The invABC genetic locus located within the Salmonella pathogenicity island 1 (SPI-1) was initially implicated in eukary otic cell invasion although invB mutants were still able to invade cells in vitro; making it difficult to assess the contribution of InvB to pathogenesis (22). invB mutants were shown to have a virulence defect in an avian model of Salmonella infection; yet the exact contribution or functional role of InvB in virulence remained un known 63). At the sequence level, InvB shares 31% sequence similarity with Spal5 (SpaK), a protein encoded in the mxi-spa genetic locus associated with Shigella host cell invasion; and there fore a link to Salmonella invasion was suggested. The first demon stration for InvB being a chaperone was a study that used a yeast two-hybrid approach and in vitro infection experiments. Bronstein et al. 6) discovered that InvB interacts with SspA (SipA) and contributes to efficient translocation of SipA into target host cells. Two groups then answered question relating to Salmonella effectors encoded outside SPI-1 by demonstrating that InvB was required for SopEand SopE2 secretion and translocation into host strain, InvB was shown to interact with SopA (20). Aside from SopE and SopE2, the InvB interacting effectors do not share extensive sequence similarity which raised the important question of InvB mediates specificity to effector partner proteins. As it turns out, intrinsic structural features within effectors are critical for InvB binding (45) (presented below). key how binding"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Spal5. The invasive enteric pathogen Shigella flexneri (and close relatives) contains a large 213-kb virulence plasmid that en codes components ofa T3SS, effectors; chaperones and transcrip tional regulators. A screen for interacting proteins encoded by the virulence plasmid identified number of potential chaperone effector associations 58). Based on homology to InvB; which was previously known as a Salmonella chaperone for the effector SipA/ SspA, a chaperone role for Spal5 was postulated based on an interaction with the effectors IpaA, IpgBI, and OspC3 58,60). This was the first evidence that T3SC could interact with multiple effectors and hence introduced the concept of promiscuous erones, chaperones that interact with multiple part ners 35). In fact; a protein interaction platform assay has since chapbinding ie revealed that Spal5 interacts with additional Shigella effectors, total ofnine) (67). Spal5 is involved in the efficient secretion of all has been implicated in an anti-activator role that negatively influ ences virulence gene transcription (61). Another observation is that to date Spal5 is the only example of a T3SC that is secreted (26). The relevance of Spal5 secretion remains to be determined.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":55.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":38.666666666666664,"r":288.3333333333333,"t":200.66666666666663},"charspan":[0,826],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":463.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":297.0,"r":546.3333333333334,"t":553.6666666666666},"charspan":[827,1227],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"Spal5. The invasive enteric pathogen Shigella flexneri (and close relatives) contains a large 213-kb virulence plasmid that en codes components ofa T3SS, effectors; chaperones and transcrip tional regulators. A screen for interacting proteins encoded by the virulence plasmid identified number of potential chaperone effector associations 58). Based on homology to InvB; which was previously known as a Salmonella chaperone for the effector SipA/ SspA, a chaperone role for Spal5 was postulated based on an interaction with the effectors IpaA, IpgBI, and OspC3 58,60). This was the first evidence that T3SC could interact with multiple effectors and hence introduced the concept of promiscuous erones, chaperones that interact with multiple part ners 35). In fact; a protein interaction platform assay has since chapbinding ie revealed that Spal5 interacts with additional Shigella effectors, total ofnine) (67). Spal5 is involved in the efficient secretion of all has been implicated in an anti-activator role that negatively influ ences virulence gene transcription (61). Another observation is that to date Spal5 is the only example of a T3SC that is secreted (26). The relevance of Spal5 secretion remains to be determined."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"CesT. CesT of enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) is perhaps one of the most unusual multicargo chaperones, as it was first described as a class IA chaperone for the effector Tir (1, 23). CesT (chaperone E. coli secreted protein Tir) fits many ofthe criteria for assignment as a class IA chaperone; the cesT gene is immediately adjacent to tir within an operon in the locus of enterocyte efface ment (LEE) pathogenicity island of attaching and effacing E. coli strains. This is similar to the cognate chaperone effector pairings for class IA chaperones seen in many pathogens SycE/YopE, that CesT binds to another LEE-encoded effector (16). The discovery of many non-LEE-encoded effectors; accompanied by the paucity of chaperone candidate genes, raised the possibility that CesT could be involved in non-LEE-encoded effector secre tion via the T3SS. Indeed this was the case, as CesT was shown to interact with all the effectors encoded within the LEE and many non-LEE-encoded effectors (73). CesT is known to interact with at least 10 effectors (Table 1) and possibly more given that some effectors are encoded by redundant genes with almost identical sequences (e.g., NleG and EspF protein families). Expectedly; cesT null mutants do not cause overt disease in mouse model of infection (18). (eg Map,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":222.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":296.3333333333333,"r":547.0,"t":465.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,1299],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"CesT. CesT of enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) is perhaps one of the most unusual multicargo chaperones, as it was first described as a class IA chaperone for the effector Tir (1, 23). CesT (chaperone E. coli secreted protein Tir) fits many ofthe criteria for assignment as a class IA chaperone; the cesT gene is immediately adjacent to tir within an operon in the locus of enterocyte efface ment (LEE) pathogenicity island of attaching and effacing E. coli strains. This is similar to the cognate chaperone effector pairings for class IA chaperones seen in many pathogens SycE/YopE, that CesT binds to another LEE-encoded effector (16). The discovery of many non-LEE-encoded effectors; accompanied by the paucity of chaperone candidate genes, raised the possibility that CesT could be involved in non-LEE-encoded effector secre tion via the T3SS. Indeed this was the case, as CesT was shown to interact with all the effectors encoded within the LEE and many non-LEE-encoded effectors (73). CesT is known to interact with at least 10 effectors (Table 1) and possibly more given that some effectors are encoded by redundant genes with almost identical sequences (e.g., NleG and EspF protein families). Expectedly; cesT null mutants do not cause overt disease in mouse model of infection (18). (eg Map,"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"HpaB. Plant pathogens within the genus Xanthomonas use T3SS to inject multiple effectors into plant cells during infection. The model organism for this group of bacteria is Xanthomonas campestris pv. vesicatoria, which causes bacterial spot disease in pepper and tomato. Studies have identified a 23-kb hypersensitive response and pathogenicity (hrp) gene cluster that contains six operons (hrpA-F) encoding components of a T3SS, effectors; reg ulators and chaperones (27). Genes without known functions or limited homology to known T3SS proteins in databases were ini tially termed hrp-associated genes (hpa). In the case of HpaB, it was shown to bind three effector proteins, AvrBsl, AvrBs3, and XopFl using in vitro binding assays (7,9). In vitro secretion assays demonstrated that five Xanthomonas effectors require HpaB for efficient secretion into culture supernatants. HpaB is required for Xanthomonas-mediated T3SS injection of two effectors into plant","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":56.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":296.3333333333333,"r":547.0,"t":222.66666666666663},"charspan":[0,960],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"HpaB. Plant pathogens within the genus Xanthomonas use T3SS to inject multiple effectors into plant cells during infection. The model organism for this group of bacteria is Xanthomonas campestris pv. vesicatoria, which causes bacterial spot disease in pepper and tomato. Studies have identified a 23-kb hypersensitive response and pathogenicity (hrp) gene cluster that contains six operons (hrpA-F) encoding components of a T3SS, effectors; reg ulators and chaperones (27). Genes without known functions or limited homology to known T3SS proteins in databases were ini tially termed hrp-associated genes (hpa). In the case of HpaB, it was shown to bind three effector proteins, AvrBsl, AvrBs3, and XopFl using in vitro binding assays (7,9). In vitro secretion assays demonstrated that five Xanthomonas effectors require HpaB for efficient secretion into culture supernatants. HpaB is required for Xanthomonas-mediated T3SS injection of two effectors into plant"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"3768","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":27.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":39.333333333333336,"r":57.333333333333336,"t":35.0},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"3768"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"jb.asm.org","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":25.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":61.0,"r":94.66666666666667,"t":35.33333333333337},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":"jb.asm.org"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"Journal of Bacteriology","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":25.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":474.6666666666667,"r":545.3333333333334,"t":36.33333333333337},"charspan":[0,23],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"Journal of Bacteriology"}],"version":"1.10.0"}